{
 "hypothesis": {
  "id": "trm-il15-relapse",
  "title": "IL-15-dependent resident memory T cells bring vitiligo back after treatment stops",
  "status": "sealed",
  "test": {
   "question": "In skin that has repigmented, does a resident-memory score measured when treatment stops predict loss of response within 52 weeks?",
   "data": "A score derived from GSE203262, using GSE169238 to define the resident-memory programs, sealed before any outcome data are seen. Applied either to a public series deposited after sealing that samples skin after treatment stops, or to 1 mm punch biopsies taken at randomization in the stop-or-step-down trial concept (docs/durability/maintenance-trial-concept.md). GSE203262 labels are resolved: !Sample_description maps '-1' to non-lesional and '-2' to lesional tissue, confirmed by 50x CXCL9/10 and 6.7x CD69 enrichment. The score is computed as S_TRM defined in docs/durability/trm-signature-preregistration.md.",
   "if_correct": "A higher score when treatment stops goes with a shorter time to loss of response: hazard ratio per standard deviation above 1, with the direction fixed before the outcome data are seen.",
   "if_incorrect": "No association, or a hazard ratio per standard deviation at or below 1.",
   "decides": "A hazard ratio above 1 leaves the memory hypothesis standing and sends the recruitment explanation to its own test. No association, with melanocyte density in the same biopsy predicting relapse instead, favours the melanocyte explanation and the hypothesis is dropped.",
   "needs_new_data": true,
   "new_data": "Skin sampled after treatment stops. GSE203262 and GSE169238 define the score but carry no relapse outcome, and no public series samples repigmented skin after withdrawal: of 34 human vitiligo series, one carries any treatment-response label (GSE256271, 8 samples). The samples must come from a series deposited later, or from 1 mm punch biopsies taken at randomization in the stop-or-step-down trial, which needs a clinical lead."
  },
